# P: fixtures the hosted compiler refuses, with or without the @[Unsafe] header, that the port or the native back end accepts.
# Each is named with the hosted compiler's first error line (checkout written as <repo>). These are the SPEC R-3 checks the
# port does not yet make (see SPEC.md R-3 and src/compiler/iyi/semantic/top_level_visitor.cr). The list may only fall: a
# fixture the run finds that is not named here fails the gate, and a name the run no longer finds is printed as a ratchet.
compile_resolve_order Error: ResolveOrderService#value is resolve_order_a's method, and this replaces it (<repo>/bench/fixtures/resolve_order_a.iyi:2). A `.iyi` file may add a method to a type it did not declare — `def blank?` on `::String` is an addition — and may not redefine one it has: the definition that goes is one other code calls, so every program built with it gets this one instead. Give it a name resolve_order_a does not use: resolve_order_a's own code calls the one it wrote (SPEC.md R-3)
cg_strings Error: String#to_unsafe is the prelude's method, and this replaces it (<repo>/src/iyi/string.iyi:224). A `.iyi` file may add a method to a type it did not declare — `def blank?` on `::String` is an addition — and may not redefine one it has: the definition that goes is one other code calls, so every program built with it gets this one instead. Give it a name the prelude does not use, or add the method to the prelude itself, where one definition answers for every program (SPEC.md R-3)
